---
/**
 * Methodology Page — Transparent explanation of mathematical algorithms,
 * CTC decomposition identity, and payroll calculation logic.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';

const title = 'Calculation Methodology & Payroll Engine Architecture';
const description = 'Detailed documentation of PayDecode\'s financial algorithms, CTC decomposition identity, EPFO wage ceiling rules, and Income-tax Act 2025 slab calculations.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: `${title} — ${site.name}`,
  description,
  url: `${site.url}/methodology`,
  inLanguage: 'en-IN',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: site.name },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/methodology"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container mt-lg mb-lg">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <span class="breadcrumb-sep">›</span>
      <span>Methodology</span>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="container pb-xxl">
    <div class="max-content-text">
      <div class="badge badge-accent mb-sm">Pure TypeScript Engine</div>
      <h1 class="display-sm mb-sm">Calculation Methodology & Architecture</h1>
      <p class="body-md text-muted mb-xl">
        Last reviewed: 17 October 2026 • Effective Tax Year 2026–27 (Income-tax Act, 2025)
      </p>

      <section class="mb-xl">
        <h2 class="title-lg mb-sm">1. Core CTC Decomposition Identity</h2>
        <p class="body-md text-body mb-base">
          Cost to Company (CTC) is a corporate financial expenditure metric, not cash salary. To model offer letters truthfully without double-counting, PayDecode defines CTC using the following strict accounting identity:
        </p>

        <div class="formula-card mb-base">
          <code>
            CTC = FixedCashCompensation + TargetVariablePay + JoiningBonus<br />
            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;+ EmployerPF + GratuityAccrual + EmployerNPS + OtherEmployerCosts
          </code>
        </div>

        <p class="body-md text-body mb-base">
          From this identity, <strong>Annual Fixed Cash Salary</strong> is computed as:
        </p>

        <div class="formula-card mb-base">
          <code>
            FixedCash = CTC - (EmployerPF + GratuityAccrual + EmployerNPS + OtherCosts) - TargetVariable - JoiningBonus
          </code>
        </div>

        <p class="body-md text-body">
          Monthly bank credit (take-home salary) is then calculated by subtracting only <em>employee-side deductions</em>:
        </p>

        <div class="formula-card mt-base">
          <code>
            MonthlyTakeHome = (FixedCash / 12) - MonthlyEmployeePF - MonthlyProfessionalTax - MonthlyTDS
          </code>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-xl">
        <h2 class="title-lg mb-sm">2. Employee Provident Fund (EPF) Rules</h2>
        <p class="body-md text-body mb-base">
          Effective <strong>17 September 2026</strong>, the Ministry of Labour and Employment updated the statutory EPFO wage ceiling from ₹15,000 to <strong>₹25,000 per month</strong> (via Gazette Notification & PIB Release).
        </p>
        <ul class="seo-list mb-base">
          <li><strong>Statutory Ceiling Scenario:</strong> PF wage base is <code>min(MonthlyBasic, ₹25,000)</code>. Employee contribution is 12% = ₹3,000/month. Employer contribution is ₹3,000/month (allocated between EPS at 8.33% and EPF at 3.67%).</li>
          <li><strong>Full Basic Scenario:</strong> Many technology enterprises deduct 12% on the entire basic salary without capping at ₹25,000. PayDecode supports this configuration explicitly.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-Double-Counting Guard:</strong> Employer PF is an employer expense already removed during fixed cash derivation; it is never deducted a second time from employee take-home pay.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-xl">
        <h2 class="title-lg mb-sm">3. Income Tax & Marginal Relief (Income-tax Act, 2025)</h2>
        <p class="body-md text-body mb-base">
          For Tax Year 2026–27, tax is calculated under the Income-tax Act, 2025:
        </p>
        <ul class="seo-list mb-base">
          <li><strong>Section 19:</strong> Standard deduction of ₹75,000 under the New Regime and ₹50,000 under the Old Regime.</li>
          <li><strong>Section 202:</strong> Slab rates of 0% (up to 4L), 5% (4L–8L), 10% (8L–12L), 15% (12L–16L), 20% (16L–20L), 25% (20L–24L), and 30% (above 24L).</li>
          <li><strong>Section 156 (Rebate & Marginal Relief):</strong> Resident individuals with taxable income up to ₹12,00,000 receive a rebate up to ₹60,000 (resulting in zero tax). For taxable income just above ₹12 Lakh, marginal relief ensures that tax after rebate does not exceed the excess income over ₹12 Lakh.</li>
          <li><strong>Health & Education Cess:</strong> 4% applied to net tax after rebate and surcharge.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-xl">
        <h2 class="title-lg mb-sm">4. Reverse Salary Engine (Binary Search)</h2>
        <p class="body-md text-body mb-base">
          Because the forward function <code>f(CTC) &rarr; MonthlyTakeHome</code> is strictly monotonically non-decreasing, the reverse calculator employs a deterministic binary search with a tolerance of &plusmn;₹100, guaranteed to converge in under 25 iterations without false-approximation shortcuts.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-xl">
        <h2 class="title-lg mb-sm">5. Engineering Scope & Exclusions</h2>
        <p class="body-md text-body mb-base">
          PayDecode is designed for ordinary Indian salaried employment compensation. The current engine deliberately excludes:
        </p>
        <ul class="seo-list">
          <li>Capital gains taxation (stocks, mutual funds, real estate).</li>
          <li>ESOP perquisite tax at vesting/exercise (governed by Fair Market Value on exercise date).</li>
          <li>Dual-employment income or mid-year employer transitions without consolidated Form 12B.</li>
          <li>Section 89 tax relief for salary arrears.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .max-content-text {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .formula-card {
    background: var(--color-surface-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--rounded-md);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .seo-list {
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }
</style>
